{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":66946131,"ns":0,"title":"Danielle Dixson","revisions":[{"revid":1385504537,"parentid":1378075765,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-11-13T16:42:04Z","size":10427,"sha1":"c0e2f8d15921ef694740a58a15ba33b89600d639","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|American ecologist}}\n{{Infobox scientist\n| fields            = Marine ecology\n| education         = [[James Cook University]]\n| thesis_title      = Smelling home: the use of olfactory cues for settlement site selection by coral reef fish larvae\n| thesis_url        = https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/29907/\n| thesis_year       = 2012\n| doctoral_advisor  = {{plainlist|\n*Philip Munday\n*Geoffrey Jones\n*Morgan Pratchett\n}}\n}}\n'''Danielle Lynn Dixson'''<ref name=\"phd\"/> is an American ecologist. She was previously an [[Associate professor|Associate Professor]] of [[Marine ecologist|Marine Ecology]] in the School of Marine Science and Policy at the [[University of Delaware]].<ref name=\":0\">{{Cite web|title=Danielle Dixson {{!}} School of Marine Science & Policy {{!}} College of Earth, Ocean and Environment {{!}} University of Delaware|url=https://www.udel.edu/academics/colleges/ceoe/departments/smsp/faculty/danielle-dixson/|access-date=2021-03-01|website=www.udel.edu|language=en|archive-date=2021-01-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210125053704/https://www.udel.edu/academics/colleges/ceoe/departments/smsp/faculty/danielle-dixson/}}</ref> Her research focusses on how human-induced change to [[marine ecosystem]]s impacts [[animal behaviour]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Danielle Dixson|url=https://500queerscientists.com/danielle-dixson/|access-date=2021-03-01|website=500 Queer Scientists|language=en-US}}</ref> Her work, now thought to be fraudulent,<ref name=\":2\" /> was about understanding how [[ocean acidification]] affects the behaviour of [[coral reef]] [[fish]]es.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-04-26|title=Danielle Dixon|url=https://www.jcu.edu.au/outstanding-alumni-awards/previous-winners/search-OA-winners/2015-outstanding-alumni-winners/danielle-dixon|access-date=2021-03-01|website=www.jcu.edu.au|language=en-AU|archive-date=2021-06-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210628170438/https://www.jcu.edu.au/outstanding-alumni-awards/previous-winners/search-OA-winners/2015-outstanding-alumni-winners/danielle-dixon}}</ref> As of 2022 her work however came under scrutiny due to inexplicable data. As of August 2026 three of her papers have been retracted <ref>{{cite web |title=Retraction Watch Database |url=https://retractiondatabase.org/RetractionSearch.aspx#?auth%3dDixson%252c%2bDanielle%2bL |website=retractiondatabase.org}}</ref>\n\n== Academic career ==\nDixson studied [[Marine Science]] at the [[University of Tampa]] in [[Florida]], obtaining her [[Bachelor of Science|B.S.]] in 2005. She went on to obtain her [[Ph.D.]] in 2012 under the supervision of Philip Munday, Geoffrey Jones, and Morgan Pratchett at [[James Cook University]], [[Australia]].<ref name=\"phd\">{{Cite thesis|title=Smelling home: the use of olfactory cues for settlement site selection by coral reef fish larvae|url=https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/29907/|publisher=James Cook University|date=August 2011|degree=phd|first=Danielle Lynn|last=Dixson}}</ref> From 2011, she worked as [[postdoctoral researcher]] at [[Georgia Institute of Technology]] until her appointment as an [[Assistant professor|Assistant Professor]] in 2013.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Danielle Dixson, Biologist Since the Tender Age of 5 {{!}} School of Biological Sciences {{!}} Georgia Institute of Technology {{!}} Atlanta, GA {{!}} Georgia Institute of Technology {{!}} Atlanta, GA|url=https://biosciences.gatech.edu/news/danielle-dixson-biologist-tender-age-5-0|access-date=2021-03-01|website=biosciences.gatech.edu|date=10 September 2018 }}</ref> In 2015 she began as an assistant professor at the University of Delaware before becoming an associate professor in 2019.<ref name=\":0\" /> She was fired from the University of Delaware in 2023 due to concerns about fraudulent research.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Quinn |first=Ryan |title=Faculty Diverges on Researcher's Alleged Misconduct |url=https://www.insidehighered.com/news/faculty-issues/research/2023/05/12/delaware-faculty-diverges-researchers-alleged-misconduct |access-date=2026-02-15 |website=Inside Higher Ed |language=en}}</ref> \n\nDixson is the author of Sea Stories, a [[Children's literature|children's book]] series based on [[scientific literature]] that aims to promote awareness of [[marine conservation]], [[Science, technology, engineering, and mathematics|STEM]] subjects, and visibility of [[Minority group|minorities]] from an early age.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Sea Story Books: Children's Books Based on REAL Science|url=https://www.seastorybooks.com/|access-date=2021-03-01|website=seastorybooks|language=en}}</ref> She is a member of the International Society of [[Chemical ecology|Chemical Ecology]] and was the recipient of their Early Career Award in 2019.<ref name=\":1\">{{Cite web|title=International Society of Chemical Ecology|url=https://www.chemecol.org/earlycareer.shtml|access-date=2021-03-01|website=www.chemecol.org|archive-date=2021-04-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210428173609/https://www.chemecol.org/earlycareer.shtml}}</ref>\n\n== Research ==\nDixson's research broadly focuses on the relationship of how marine animals sense their environment and how this influences their decisions.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Dr. Danielle Dixson|url=http://www.dixsonlab.com/dr-danielle-dixson|access-date=2021-03-01|website=The Dixson Lab|language=en-US|archive-date=2021-06-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210628164937/http://www.dixsonlab.com/dr-danielle-dixson}}</ref> Her major research areas include the role of chemical cues in corals and coral reef fishes, and the influence of ocean acidification on fish behaviour.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Research|url=http://www.dixsonlab.com/research|access-date=2021-03-01|website=The Dixson Lab|language=en-US|archive-date=2021-06-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210628164938/http://www.dixsonlab.com/research}}</ref>\n\n== Misconduct allegations ==\nDixson has been accused of fabricating primary data by other researchers in the field and is the subject of ongoing institutional investigations.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Does ocean acidification alter fish behavior? Fraud allegations create a sea of doubt\n|url=https://www.science.org/content/article/does-ocean-acidification-alter-fish-behavior-fraud-allegations-create-sea-doubt|access-date=2021-06-10|website=Science Magazine|language=en-US}}</ref> On August 9, 2022, ''[[Science (journal)|Science]]'' published a piece announcing that an investigation by the University of Delaware found Dixson guilty of scientific misconduct, including data fabrication and falsification related to her work on coral reef fish behaviour.<ref name=\":2\">{{Cite web |last=Enserink |first=Martin |date=2022 |title=Star marine ecologist committed misconduct, university says |url=https://www.science.org/content/article/star-marine-ecologist-committed-misconduct-university-says |access-date=Aug 9, 2022 |website=Science}}</ref> A separate investigation is reportedly being conducted by the Georgia Institute of Technology.<ref name=\":2\" /> In 2022 a first retraction happened for Dixson<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Dixson |first1=Danielle L. |last2=Abrego |first2=David |last3=Hay |first3=Mark E. |title=RETRACTED: Chemically mediated behavior of recruiting corals and fishes: A tipping point that may limit reef recovery |journal=Science |date=22 August 2014 |volume=345 |issue=6199 |pages=892–897 |doi=10.1126/science.1255057 |pmid=25146281 |pmc=4470392 |bibcode=2014Sci...345..892D }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1126/science.ade2691|pmid=35943365|https://retractionwatch.com/2022/08/09/science-retracts-ocean-acidification-paper-more-than-a-year-after-a-report-on-allegations-in-its-own-pages/ ''Retraction Watch''|intentional=yes}}</ref> The next year; a second paper that Dixson originally published in 2016 was retracted on January 16th, 2023.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Proceedings B Editorial team |date=2023-06-28 |title=Retraction of: 'Reef fishes can recognize bleached habitat during settlement: sea anemone bleaching alters anemonefish host selection' (2016) by Scott and Dixson |journal=Proceedings. Biological Sciences |volume=290 |issue=2001 |article-number=20231346 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2023.1346 |issn=1471-2954 |pmc=10297828 |pmid=37368862}}</ref> In 2026 another paper was retracted due to statistically implausible variance in the data<ref>{{cite journal |title=RETRACTION : Ocean Acidification Disrupts the Innate Ability of Fish to Detect Predator Olfactory Cues |journal=Ecology Letters |date=August 2026 |volume=29 |issue=8 |article-number=e70445 |doi=10.1111/ele.70445 |pmid=42556795 |pmc=13441514 }}</ref>\n\n== Honours and awards ==\n\n* International Society of Chemical Ecology Early Career Award<ref name=\":1\" />\n* [[National Science Foundation]] (NSF) Faculty Early Career Development Award<ref>{{Cite web|title=UD's Danielle Dixson wins NSF Career Award|url=https://www.udel.edu/udaily/2018/july/danielle-dixson-national-science-foundation-career-award-clownfish/|access-date=2021-03-01|website=www.udel.edu|language=en}}</ref>\n* James Cook University Outstanding Alumni Award<ref>{{Cite web|title=James Cook University|url=https://alumni.jcu.edu.au/pages/outstanding-alumni-awards/previous-years/2015/2015-alumni-awards/2015-outstanding-alumni--|access-date=2021-03-01|website=alumni.jcu.edu.au|archive-date=2019-03-09|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190309204210/https://alumni.jcu.edu.au/pages/outstanding-alumni-awards/previous-years/2015/2015-alumni-awards/2015-outstanding-alumni--}}</ref>\n\n==See also==\n* [[ List_of_scientific_misconduct_incidents#Other | Oona Lönnstedt]] See misconduct case 